WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.842
A two-layer network can be differentiated by tracing the same small operations used in the forward

00:00:06.842 --> 00:00:13.484
pass. Save values such as the preactivation, hidden activation and prediction. Their local derivatives then connect

00:00:13.484 --> 00:00:16.390
the scalar loss back to each parameter.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:23.093
Save the preactivation as well as the activation. Its sign determines which local derivative will be

00:00:23.093 --> 00:00:23.480
used.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.740
The output layer multiplies the hidden activation by two. The target is held fixed at three.

00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:35.619
Can this example have a large loss but zero gradient through its first layer? Pause here

00:00:35.619 --> 00:00:37.627
and make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:46.622
Each factor comes from one edge: loss, output weight, activation and input multiplication. Omitting a factor

00:00:46.622 --> 00:00:54.510
changes the gradient. For our starting inputs, Hidden activation: 1. Prediction: 2. Loss: 0.5. Weight gradient:

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:55.003
-4.

00:00:55.533 --> 00:01:02.579
For negative preactivation, this example passes zero gradient through ReLU even when loss is large. At

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:08.303
exactly zero we use the implementation convention of zero, not an ordinary derivative.

00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:14.331
A gradient is a sensitivity of a stated loss to a stated parameter. Trace its path

00:01:14.331 --> 00:01:17.423
and account for every branch before applying an update.

